---
/**
 * The homepage, rebuilt as an argument.
 *
 * It used to be a catalogue: hero, work, clients, three capability cards,
 * numbers, campaigns, a studio note, a CTA. Every agency site has those
 * sections, and none of them is a sentence a visitor could repeat to a
 * colleague afterwards. The page stated capabilities where it needed to state
 * a belief.
 *
 * So it now runs one case, in order: the claim, the work as evidence, where the
 * name comes from, what actually breaks, the outcomes, what we are wrong for,
 * how the work runs, and one named practice. The proof sections that already
 * earned their place — the wall, the client row, the outcomes, the campaign
 * strip — are the evidence inside it rather than items in a list.
 *
 * The capability cards are gone. They were the clearest instance of the problem
 * (three columns of things we can do), the method section covers the same
 * ground as part of the argument, and /services holds the detail for anyone who
 * wants it. `HeroStatement` is still in the tree, one import swap away.
 */
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import SiteHeader from '../components/SiteHeader.astro';
import SiteFooter from '../components/SiteFooter.astro';
import HeroSeam from '../components/HeroSeam.astro';
import Argument from '../components/Argument.astro';
import WorkWall from '../components/WorkWall.astro';
import ImpactCarousel from '../components/ImpactCarousel.astro';
import CampaignStrip from '../components/CampaignStrip.astro';
import ClientMarquee from '../components/ClientMarquee.astro';
import { HONEST, PROCESS, SITE } from '../data/site';
---

<Base>
  <SiteHeader />

  <main id="main">
    <HeroSeam />

    <!-- The claim, then the evidence, before any of the reasoning. -->
    <section class="border-t border-rule px-6 pt-12 md:px-20 md:pt-18">
      <p class="font-display max-w-[46ch] text-[22px] leading-[1.4] tracking-[-0.015em] text-pretty md:text-[27px]">
        A short piece on why products break in the same place every time, and what
        one team standing on both sides of it does about that. But first, some work:
      </p>
    </section>
    <WorkWall />

    <ClientMarquee />

    <Argument
      id="origin"
      statement="<span lang='yo'>Ojú</span> means <em>face</em>.<br /><span lang='yo' style='white-space:nowrap'>Ojú-ìwé</span> means <em>page</em>."
      kicker="Where the name comes from"
    >
      <p>
        In Yoruba, <span lang="yo"><em>ojú</em></span> is the face, and also the eye.
        <span lang="yo"><em>Ojú-ìwé</em></span> &mdash; the face of a leaf &mdash; is how
        you say page. The word for a surface someone reads is built from the word for
        the part of a person that does the reading.
      </p>
      <p class="keep">
        That is an interface, described precisely, a very long time before anyone
        needed the English word. A face is not decoration laid over a body. It is
        where a thing meets you, and it is honest about what sits behind it.
      </p>
      <p>
        We took the name because we build both halves &mdash; and because a studio
        that only drew the face would have no business using it.
      </p>
    </Argument>

    <Argument
      id="thesis"
      statement="Nothing breaks in the middle of a screen."
      kicker="What actually goes wrong"
    >
      <p>
        The happy path gets drawn, reviewed, approved and built. It is nearly always
        fine. What fails is everything at the join: the empty state nobody specced,
        the error copy written in a ticket at 6pm, the loading behaviour that was a
        designer's assumption and a developer's guess, and the fourteen edge cases
        that only exist once real data arrives.
      </p>
      <p>
        None of those are design problems, and none of them are engineering problems.
        They are handover problems &mdash; invisible to whichever side does not own them.
      </p>

      <div class="split">
        <div class="split__col">
          <p class="label">What gets drawn</p>
          <p class="split__text">Every screen in the deck, in the states somebody thought of.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="split__col split__col--accent">
          <p class="label split__label--accent">What gets reached</p>
          <p class="split__text">
            Every state the product can actually enter, including the ones nobody drew.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </Argument>

    <!-- The outcomes stand as the proof of the beat above. -->
    <ImpactCarousel />

    <!-- Honest limits. -->
    <section class="border-t border-rule px-6 py-14 md:px-20 md:py-21" aria-labelledby="honest">
      <p class="label" id="honest">Being straight about it</p>
      <div class="mt-9 grid gap-10 md:mt-12 md:grid-cols-2 md:gap-20">
        <div>
          <h2 class="font-display text-[26px] leading-[1.15] tracking-[-0.025em] md:text-[30px]">
            Worth hiring us for
          </h2>
          <ul class="honest">
            {HONEST.good.map((line) => <li>{line}</li>)}
          </ul>
        </div>
        <div>
          <h2 class="font-display text-soft text-[26px] leading-[1.15] tracking-[-0.025em] md:text-[30px]">
            Not worth hiring us for
          </h2>
          <ul class="honest honest--muted">
            {HONEST.bad.map((line) => <li>{line}</li>)}
          </ul>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- How the work runs. Same four stages as /services, stated as method. -->
    <section class="border-t border-rule px-6 py-14 md:px-20 md:py-21" aria-labelledby="method">
      <div class="md:grid md:grid-cols-[minmax(0,5fr)_minmax(0,6fr)] md:gap-16 xl:gap-24">
        <div>
          <h2 id="method" class="method__head font-display">
            Draw it. Build it.<br />Ship it. Stay.
          </h2>
          <p class="label mt-4 md:mt-5">How the work runs</p>
        </div>
        <p class="mt-6 max-w-[52ch] text-base leading-relaxed text-soft md:mt-1 md:text-[17px]">
          Four stages, one team across all of them. The people who draw it are the
          people who build it, which is the only reason the join holds.
        </p>
      </div>

      <ol class="steps">
        {
          PROCESS.map((step) => (
            <li class="step">
              <span class="font-mono text-[11px] tracking-[0.16em] text-accent">{step.num}</span>
              <h3 class="font-display mt-3 text-[20px] tracking-[-0.02em] md:text-[22px]">
                {step.title}
              </h3>
              <p class="mt-2.5 text-[15px] leading-[1.55] text-soft">{step.body}</p>
            </li>
          ))
        }
      </ol>
    </section>

    <CampaignStrip />

    <Argument
      id="seam-review"
      statement="The Seam Review"
      kicker="One thing we do that most studios do not"
      tone="fill"
    >
      <p>
        Before anything ships we walk the whole product looking only for joins:
        every state a screen can enter, every place a design decision quietly became
        a developer's judgement call, every string written in a hurry and never read
        again.
      </p>
      <p class="keep">
        It takes half a day. It is where most of the bugs a user would have found
        instead get found.
      </p>
      <p>
        We will run one on a product we did not build.
        <a href="/contact" class="inline-cta">Ask about it &rarr;</a>
      </p>
    </Argument>

    <!-- CONTACT -->
    <section class="border-t border-rule px-6 py-16 md:px-20 md:py-28">
      <h2 class="font-display max-w-[1000px] text-[44px] leading-[1.04] tracking-[-0.022em] md:text-[88px] md:leading-[1.02] md:tracking-[-0.028em]">
        Tell us where it is breaking.
      </h2>
      <p class="mt-6 max-w-[46ch] text-base leading-relaxed text-soft md:mt-8 md:text-[17px]">
        Bring the part that keeps going wrong. We would rather start there than with
        a brief.
      </p>
      <div class="mt-9 flex flex-col items-start gap-7 md:mt-14 md:flex-row md:items-end md:justify-between md:gap-16">
        <a href="/contact" class="cta">
          <span class="text-base md:text-[17px]">Start a conversation</span>
          <span class="font-mono text-[15px]" aria-hidden="true">→</span>
        </a>
        <a href={`mailto:${SITE.email}`} class="font-mono text-[13px] text-soft hover:text-ink md:text-sm">
          {SITE.email}
        </a>
      </div>
    </section>
  </main>

  <SiteFooter />
</Base>

<style>
  /* drawn vs reached — the thesis, stated twice in four words */
  .split {
    display: grid;
    gap: 1.5rem;
    margin-top: 0.75rem;
  }
  @media (min-width: 640px) {
    .split { grid-template-columns: repeat(2, minmax(0, 1fr)); gap: 2rem; }
  }
  .split__col {
    border-top: 1px solid var(--color-ink);
    padding-top: 1rem;
  }
  .split__col--accent {
    border-top-color: var(--color-accent);
  }
  .split__label--accent {
    color: var(--color-accent);
  }
  .split__text {
    margin-top: 0.75rem;
    font-size: 0.9375rem;
    line-height: 1.55;
    color: var(--color-ink);
  }

  /* honest limits */
  .honest {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: 0.875rem;
    margin-top: 1.375rem;
    list-style: none;
    padding: 0;
    font-size: 1rem;
    line-height: 1.6;
    text-wrap: pretty;
  }
  .honest--muted {
    color: var(--color-soft);
  }

  /* method */
  /* Sized like the argument statements rather than by hand. At a fixed 44px in
     a 5fr column this broke as "Draw it. Build / it." — the forced line break
     only holds if the line before it fits. */
  .method__head {
    font-size: clamp(1.875rem, 4.2vw, 2.75rem);
    line-height: 1.1;
    letter-spacing: -0.03em;
    text-wrap: balance;
  }

  .steps {
    display: grid;
    gap: 2rem 1.5rem;
    margin: 2.75rem 0 0;
    padding: 0;
    list-style: none;
    counter-reset: none;
  }
  @media (min-width: 640px) {
    .steps { grid-template-columns: repeat(2, minmax(0, 1fr)); }
  }
  @media (min-width: 1024px) {
    .steps { grid-template-columns: repeat(4, minmax(0, 1fr)); gap: 1.5rem; margin-top: 3.5rem; }
  }
  .step {
    border-top: 1px solid var(--color-rule);
    padding-top: 1.125rem;
  }

  .inline-cta {
    color: var(--color-ink);
    text-decoration: none;
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--color-rule);
    transition: border-color 200ms ease;
    white-space: nowrap;
  }
  .inline-cta:hover,
  .inline-cta:focus-visible {
    border-color: var(--color-ink);
  }
</style>
